[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m ### Step 2: Analyze the candidate graph
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m #### Graph:
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m - A → C  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m - A → D  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m - B → C  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m - C → D
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m This is a directed acyclic graph (DAG), and the causal relationships are:
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m - A → C → D  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m - B → C → D  
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m - So, A and B both influence D, and both influence C, which in turn influences D.
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m Thus, **A and B are ancestors of D**, and **A and B are ancestors of C**, and **C is ancestor of D**.
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m So, for any node:
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m - A → C → D
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m - B → C → D
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m So, **D is a descendant of A and B**, and **C is a descendant of A and B**.
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m So, perturbing A leads to D being a descendant.
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m 
[36m(RewardLoopWorker pid=3660498)[0m ---
